<BaseLayout
  title="Deductibles explained"
  description="What an insurance deductible is, and how it trades off against your premium."
>
  <a class="back-link" href="/learn/">&larr; All guides</a>
  <div class="prose">
    <h1>Deductibles: what they are and how they trade off</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      A deductible is the amount you pay out of pocket before your insurance
      starts paying on a claim. Choosing a deductible is really choosing how
      much risk you keep yourself versus how much you pay the insurer to take
      on.
    </p>

    <h2>The basic trade-off</h2>
    <p>
      A higher deductible generally means a lower premium, because you're
      agreeing to cover more of a claim yourself. A lower deductible generally
      means a higher premium. Neither is automatically the "right" choice —
      it depends on how much you could comfortably pay out of pocket if you
      had a claim tomorrow.
    </p>

    <h2>Auto deductibles</h2>
    <p>
      Collision and comprehensive coverage each carry their own deductible,
      often chosen separately. A useful check: if your deductible is close to
      or more than half of your vehicle's value, the most that coverage could
      ever pay out is capped fairly low — worth weighing against what you're
      paying in premium for it.
    </p>

    <h2>Home deductibles</h2>
    <p>
      Home deductibles are sometimes a flat dollar amount, and sometimes a
      percentage of your dwelling coverage limit instead — which can add up
      to a much larger number than it first appears. For example, Florida
      requires insurers to offer hurricane deductible options that can be a
      flat $500 or a percentage (2%, 5%, or 10%) of the dwelling limit,
      applied separately from your regular deductible — see
      <a href="/home-insurance/fl/">Florida's verified home insurance rules</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Percentage-based wind or hurricane deductibles are common in
      hurricane-exposed states generally, but the exact rule depends on the
      state and the policy — always check your own declarations page rather
      than assuming a percentage. Texas coastal homes have a related but
      different issue: some home policies exclude wind and hail entirely in
      coastal areas, with separate coverage sold through TWIA — see
      <a href="/home-insurance/tx/">Texas's verified home insurance rules</a>.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
      <p>
        A percentage deductible can be much larger in dollars than it sounds.
        On a $400,000 dwelling limit, a 2% deductible is $8,000 — due before
        your insurer pays anything on that claim. Do the math in dollars, not
        just the percentage, before deciding it's workable.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p>
      Enter your own numbers into the <a href="/tools/">Coverage Gap
      Checker</a> to see this calculated against your actual dwelling limit
      or vehicle value.
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
